\title{Data hazards stall when an instruction needs a not-yet-written value}

\p{When one instruction's result is the next instruction's input, a naive [pipeline](COV5) would have to stall: the second instruction can't execute until the first has written back. Data hazards are the most pervasive source of stalls in a CPU.}

\p{Two standard mitigations:}
\ul{
  \li{[[USSP]] — route the producer's output directly into the consumer's input, before the writeback stage commits it. The consumer sees the result one cycle earlier.}
  \li{[register renaming](5CT9) — false dependencies, where two instructions write to the same architectural register but operate on independent values, can be broken by mapping the architectural register to two different physical registers.}
}
